# Copy to .env for local development. .env is gitignored; this file is not.
#
# EVERYTHING HERE IS OPTIONAL. With none of it set, the app runs against a local
# libSQL file with no network — which is how development and the whole test suite
# run.

# --- database -----------------------------------------------------------------
# DB_PATH=./data/alfred.db

# --- Turso, and why it is NOT in .env -----------------------------------------
# Setting TURSO_URL turns DB_PATH into an embedded replica of a REAL database.
# Bun loads `.env` automatically in every process started in this directory — the
# app, the test suite, and any throwaway script — so credentials placed here are
# credentials every one of those processes silently gets.
#
# That is not hypothetical. The browser suite once ran this way and wrote ~200
# rows into production; see `.plan/changes-v10.md`.
#
# So they live in `.env.turso` (gitignored) and nothing loads it unless asked:
#
#     bun --env-file=.env.turso run user:add owner
#     DB_PATH=/tmp/check.db bun --env-file=.env.turso src/server/index.ts
#
# .env.turso holds exactly two lines:
#     TURSO_URL=libsql://your-db.turso.io
#     TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN=...

# --- auth ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is nothing to configure. Every request needs a valid session cookie,
# always — in development as much as in production — so there is no flag to set
# and no way to deploy this with the door open.
#
# Create an account before you can sign in. There is no signup page:
#
#     echo 'a-long-enough-password' | bun run user:add owner
#
# `owner` is user 1, created by the migration, and owns everything written
# before accounts existed. The same command sets an existing user's password,
# which also invalidates their existing sessions.

# PORT=3000
